 the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Burns' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Burns has set <strong>161 standing record lows</strong>, more than the 112 that chance alone would give, and <strong>165 record highs</strong>, more than the 112 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (85). Burns' all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on July 12, 2002.","t_chg":"Burns' nights cooled over the long run. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 82 complete years: Burns' average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.62 to \u22120.34), about \u22124.1\u00b0F since 1939. Burns' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.33), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1939.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 9 months fell. Highs rose in 4 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Burns' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.16 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 10.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (18.24 in); the driest, 1949 (5.32 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 6 daily record lows"}}